import json

NEW_PAIRS = [
  {"question": "What causes a P0171 code?", "answer": "P0171 means system too lean (bank 1). Common causes: vacuum leak (intake manifold, hoses, PCV), dirty/failing MAF sensor, weak fuel pump, clogged fuel filter, faulty O2 sensor, leaking fuel injector O-rings, or exhaust leak before the O2 sensor. Start by checking for vacuum leaks with carb cleaner (spray around intake, if idle changes, you found it) and clean the MAF."},
  {"question": "What causes a P0420 code?", "answer": "P0420 means catalyst system efficiency below threshold (bank 1). Causes: aging/failed catalytic converter, downstream O2 sensor failure, exhaust leak between the engine and the rear O2 sensor, or an engine running rich/oil-burning for a long time (which poisons the cat). First check the O2 sensor output, look for exhaust leaks, and verify the engine isn't misfiring. If all else is fine, the cat is likely due for replacement."},
  {"question": "What is a P0442 code?", "answer": "P0442 means a small EVAP system leak detected. The EVAP system captures fuel vapors. A small leak is usually the gas cap (loose, missing, or with a bad seal), or a cracked rubber hose in the EVAP system, or a faulty purge/vent valve. Tighten the gas cap, clear the code, and drive 2-3 drive cycles. If it returns, smoke test the EVAP system."},
  {"question": "What causes a P0301 code?", "answer": "P0301 means cylinder 1 misfire. Diagnose by swapping parts: 1) Swap cylinder 1 spark plug with another cylinder, if misfire moves, plug is bad. 2) Swap the ignition coil, if it moves, coil is bad. 3) If misfire stays on cylinder 1, check fuel injector (swap with another cylinder too), then compression (a compression test will show if the cylinder is low, indicating valve or ring issues)."},
  {"question": "What is the difference between a misfire and a backfire?", "answer": "A misfire is a failure of combustion in a cylinder (no ignition, no power). A backfire is combustion happening at the wrong time, either in the intake (intake backfire) or exhaust (exhaust backfire). Misfires cause rough running, check engine light, and unburned fuel in the exhaust. Backfires are usually caused by timing problems, lean/rich conditions, or valve issues."},
  {"question": "What is the purpose of a PCV valve?", "answer": "The PCV (Positive Crankcase Ventilation) valve recirculates blowby gases from the crankcase back into the intake to be burned. This reduces emissions and prevents crankcase pressure buildup. A stuck-open PCV valve causes rough idle; a stuck-closed one causes oil leaks, sludge buildup, and pressure-related seal failures. Check by removing it and shaking, it should rattle."},
  {"question": "What is a MAP sensor?", "answer": "The MAP (Manifold Absolute Pressure) sensor measures the pressure inside the intake manifold. Combined with intake air temperature, the ECU calculates airflow into the engine. Symptoms of failure: rough idle, poor fuel economy, hesitation, stalling, black smoke, check engine light (P0105-P0109). Located on the intake manifold or firewall."},
  {"question": "What is a knock sensor?", "answer": "The knock sensor detects engine knock (premature detonation) via a piezo-electric element mounted on the engine block. It sends a signal to the ECU, which retards ignition timing to prevent damage. Symptoms of failure: reduced power, poor fuel economy, check engine light (P0325-P0332), and audible knocking under load."},
  {"question": "What is a crankshaft position sensor?", "answer": "The crankshaft position sensor (CKP) tells the ECU the exact position and speed of the crankshaft. Without it, the ECU doesn't know when to fire the spark or inject fuel. Symptoms of failure: engine won't start, stalls intermittently, rough running, or a check engine light (P0335-P0339). Often fails when hot and works when cooled (a classic intermittent stall)."},
  {"question": "What causes a car to not start?", "answer": "No-start diagnosis: 1) Check battery, 12.6V is healthy. 2) Listen for starter click. 3) Check for fuel (fuel pump hum). 4) Check for spark. 5) Check immobilizer. 6) Check for security system lockout. Always start with the battery and work backwards."},
  {"question": "What is blow-by and why does it matter?", "answer": "Blow-by is combustion gas escaping past the piston rings into the crankcase. Excessive blow-by indicates worn rings or cylinder walls. Test: remove the oil cap with the engine running, if there's a strong puffing stream of air, rings are worn. Symptoms: oil in the intake, oil consumption, blue smoke from exhaust, and low compression."},
  {"question": "What is hydraulic lifter tick?", "answer": "A hydraulic lifter tick is a rhythmic tapping noise from the top of the engine, especially at cold start. Hydraulic lifters use oil pressure to maintain valve clearance. If oil isn't reaching them, they tick. Causes: old/dirty oil, low oil pressure, worn lifters, or a clogged oil passage."},
  {"question": "What is a timing chain vs timing belt?", "answer": "A timing belt is rubber and needs replacement every 60,000-100,000 miles. A timing chain is metal and typically lasts the life of the engine, but can stretch if oil changes are neglected. If a chain or belt fails, it's catastrophic on interference engines, valves hit pistons."},
  {"question": "What is a variable valve timing (VVT) system?", "answer": "VVT changes when valves open and close based on RPM and load. This improves power, efficiency, and emissions. The ECU controls oil pressure to a phaser that rotates the camshaft. Symptoms of VVT failure: rough idle, reduced power, poor economy, rattle at startup, and codes like P0010-P0015."},
  {"question": "What does a leaking valve cover gasket cause?", "answer": "A leaking valve cover gasket allows oil to escape from the top of the engine. Symptoms: oil around the valve cover, burning oil smell, and oil fouling of spark plugs. Tighten bolts first, if that doesn't fix it, replace the gasket."},
  {"question": "What is the difference between SOHC and DOHC?", "answer": "SOHC has one camshaft per cylinder head; DOHC has two. DOHC allows more valves per cylinder, better breathing, and independent intake/exhaust timing for more power and efficiency. DOHC is more complex and expensive."},
  {"question": "What is a turbocharger wastegate?", "answer": "The wastegate controls boost pressure by diverting exhaust gas away from the turbine once target boost is reached. If it sticks open, you get no/low boost. If it sticks closed, you overboost, which is dangerous. Symptoms: overboost codes (P0234), limp mode, or no boost at all."},
  {"question": "What is a blow-off valve?", "answer": "A blow-off valve (BOV) releases boost pressure from the intake when the throttle closes. Recirculation valves recirculate air back into the intake instead of atmosphere. BOVs that vent to atmosphere can cause rich running on MAF-based cars."},
  {"question": "What is detonation vs pre-ignition?", "answer": "Detonation (knock) is when the air-fuel mixture ignites from pressure and heat AFTER the spark. Pre-ignition is when the mixture ignites BEFORE the spark, usually from a hot spot. Pre-ignition is more dangerous, it can melt pistons. Both are prevented by correct octane, cool intake temps, and proper tuning."},
  {"question": "What is a head gasket?", "answer": "The head gasket sits between the engine block and cylinder head. It seals combustion chambers, coolant passages, and oil passages. A blown head gasket causes: overheating, white smoke from exhaust, milky oil, bubbles in radiator, and loss of compression. Repair costs $1,500-$3,000+."},
  {"question": "What is a compression test?", "answer": "A compression test measures how much pressure each cylinder can build. Remove all spark plugs, screw a compression gauge into a spark plug hole, crank 5-8 times, and read the gauge. Healthy cylinders build 150-200 psi. A cylinder 20%+ lower than the others is a problem. Add oil to a low cylinder: if compression improves, rings are worn; if not, valves are the issue."},
  {"question": "What is a leak-down test?", "answer": "A leak-down test measures how much air escapes from a pressurized cylinder. Bring the cylinder to TDC, pressurize with compressed air, and measure the percentage lost. Listen for escaping air: intake (intake valve), exhaust (exhaust valve), oil filler (rings), radiator (head gasket). Healthy engines leak less than 10%."},
  {"question": "What does a faulty EGR valve cause?", "answer": "A stuck-open EGR valve causes rough idle, stalling, and hesitation. A stuck-closed EGR valve causes engine knock/pinging under load. Symptoms: rough idle, check engine light (P0400-P0406), and pinging. Fix: clean or replace the valve."},
  {"question": "What is a radiator pressure test?", "answer": "A radiator pressure test checks for leaks in the cooling system. A pump is attached to the radiator cap, the system is pressurized to the cap rating, and you watch for pressure drop. A drop indicates a leak. Bubbles with the engine running can indicate a head gasket failure."},
  {"question": "What is the purpose of a thermostat?", "answer": "The thermostat controls coolant flow to maintain optimal engine temperature. When cold, it's closed and coolant warms up faster. When hot, it opens and coolant flows through the radiator. A stuck-closed thermostat causes overheating. A stuck-open thermostat causes the engine to run too cold."},
  {"question": "What causes a car to idle too high?", "answer": "High idle causes: vacuum leaks (most common), dirty throttle body, faulty idle air control valve, coolant temp sensor failure, throttle position sensor failure, or a sticking throttle cable. Start with: check for vacuum leaks, clean the throttle body, and scan for codes."},
  {"question": "What is a lean vs rich condition?", "answer": "Lean = too much air, not enough fuel (P0171). Symptoms: hesitation, misfire, high idle, backfire. Rich = too much fuel, not enough air (P0172). Symptoms: black smoke, fouled plugs, poor fuel economy. Long-term lean or rich conditions can damage the engine."},
  {"question": "What is a fuel pressure test?", "answer": "A fuel pressure test measures the fuel pressure at the rail. Attach a gauge to the test port, turn the key on, and read the pressure. Compare to spec. Low pressure indicates a weak pump, clogged filter, or faulty regulator. Pressure drops after shutoff means a leaking injector or check valve."},
  {"question": "What is the purpose of an exhaust manifold?", "answer": "The exhaust manifold collects exhaust gases from each cylinder and directs them into a single pipe (or turbo). A cracked exhaust manifold causes a tick/tapping sound that's louder when cold. Symptoms: ticking noise, exhaust smell in the engine bay."},
  {"question": "What is an idle air control (IAC) valve?", "answer": "The IAC valve allows the ECU to control idle RPM by bypassing a small amount of air around the throttle plate. Symptoms of failure: rough idle, stalling, surging, or high idle. Often fixed by cleaning with throttle body cleaner."},
  {"question": "What is a throttle position sensor (TPS)?", "answer": "The TPS tells the ECU the position of the throttle plate. The ECU uses this to calculate fuel, timing, and transmission shift points. Symptoms of failure: poor acceleration, hesitation, stalling, rough idle, or a check engine light (P0120-P0124)."},
  {"question": "What is a high-pressure fuel pump (HPFP)?", "answer": "Gasoline Direct Injection (GDI) engines use a high-pressure fuel pump to pressurize fuel to 1,500-3,000 psi. The HPFP is driven by the camshaft. Symptoms of failure: long cranking, hesitation, misfire, reduced power, and codes like P0087 (fuel rail pressure too low)."},
  {"question": "What causes a car to shudder at highway speeds?", "answer": "Highway shudder causes: unbalanced tires (most common), bent wheel, worn CV axles, bad U-joints, warped brake rotors (if only when braking), or worn suspension. Start with a road force balance on the tires."},
  {"question": "What is an oil pressure warning light?", "answer": "The oil pressure warning light means oil pressure is below safe levels. Stop the engine immediately. Causes: low oil level, wrong oil viscosity, clogged oil filter, failed oil pump, or worn bearings. If the light comes on, pull over safely and shut off the engine."},
  {"question": "What is a serpentine belt?", "answer": "The serpentine belt drives multiple accessories: alternator, power steering pump, water pump, A/C compressor, and sometimes the supercharger. Symptoms of failure: squealing, cracking or glazing on the belt, missing ribs. A broken serpentine belt causes loss of power steering and engine overheating within minutes."},
  {"question": "What is the purpose of engine oil?", "answer": "Engine oil serves five functions: 1) Lubricates moving parts. 2) Cools components. 3) Cleans the engine. 4) Seals gaps. 5) Protects against corrosion. Synthetic oil resists breakdown better than conventional and flows better in cold temperatures."},
  {"question": "What is a cylinder head?", "answer": "The cylinder head sits on top of the engine block, sealing the cylinders. It contains: combustion chamber, valves, spark plugs or injectors, camshaft(s), and coolant/oil passages. A warped head (from overheating) can cause head gasket failure."},
  {"question": "What is a valve job?", "answer": "A valve job is a repair where the cylinder head is removed, and the valves and valve seats are re-ground to restore a proper seal. Typically $500-$1,500 depending on the engine."},
  {"question": "What is engine vacuum and why does it matter?", "answer": "Engine vacuum is the pressure difference between the intake manifold and atmosphere. A healthy engine at idle pulls 18-22 inHg. Low or fluctuating vacuum indicates: vacuum leak, worn rings, valve issues, or timing problems."},
  {"question": "What is a misfire counter?", "answer": "A misfire counter shows how many misfires have occurred on each cylinder. A cylinder with thousands of misfires is the problem. Use it to guide diagnosis: swap coils/plugs between a bad and good cylinder to isolate the fault."},
  {"question": "What is a top engine cleaner?", "answer": "A top engine cleaner is a chemical sprayed into the intake to remove carbon buildup from the intake valves, combustion chamber, and EGR system. Carbon buildup is a common issue on direct injection engines. The cleaner is sprayed through the intake while the engine runs."},
  {"question": "What is a cylinder leak-down test?", "answer": "A leak-down test measures how much air escapes from a cylinder when pressurized. It's more precise than a compression test for locating the source of a leak. Bring the cylinder to TDC, pressurize, and measure the percentage lost. Less than 10% is healthy."}
]

